// @NAME: Getting Started
// @TITLE: Fetching, streaming and framing bytes — and why text cannot carry them.
//
// `web` is the client half: HTTP requests (`http_get` and friends, including
// byte-range reads of a large remote file), a WebSocket client that reconnects
// on its own, and a small binary packer for the frames you send over it.
//
// It is not the server — that is `server`, which is built on this package and
// whose guide does a live round trip between the two. It is not a browser DOM
// binding either; on wasm the same calls are routed through the host's
// `fetch()` and `WebSocket`, so a loft program compiled for the browser writes
// the code below unchanged.
//
// ⚠ This guide runs WITHOUT a network, exactly as the package's CI does — the
// live HTTP examples live in `tests-network/http.loft`. That is not a gap being
// papered over: the contracts worth teaching here are what a call answers when
// the other end is NOT there, and having no server is what makes them
// demonstrable.
use web;

fn main() {
  a_request_that_cannot_reach_anything();

  // Every section is a function, and `main` calls each one. A section `main`
  // does not call still COMPILES — so a renamed API is caught, and the
  // assertions that check the example is RIGHT never run.
  a_handle_is_not_a_connection();
  frames_that_carry_a_zero_byte();
  reading_one_byte_back();
}

// ## An unreachable server is an ANSWER, not an error
//
// `http_get` returns an `HttpResponse { status, body, headers }` whatever
// happens. A server that is down, not started yet, or briefly unreachable
// answers **status 0** — so the shape of a retry is a plain loop over the
// status, and there is no error path to write.
//
// `response.ok()` is the 2xx test, and it is false for 0 as it is for 404.

fn a_request_that_cannot_reach_anything() {
  r = http_get("http://127.0.0.1:18999/nothing");   // nothing listens there
  assert(r.status == 0, "an unreachable server answers 0, got {r.status}");
  assert(!r.ok(), "…and ok() is false, exactly as it is for a 404");
  assert(len(r.body) == 0, "…with an empty body");

  n = http_size("http://127.0.0.1:18999/nothing");
  assert(n == -1, "http_size answers -1 when the length is unavailable, got {n}");

  println("http   : unreachable = status 0, size -1 — answers, never errors");
}

// That is the whole error model. A reachable server fills the same three fields:
//
//     r = http_get("https://example.com/");
//     if r.ok() { println(r.body); }
//
// `http_post` / `http_put` / `http_delete` take a body; every one of them has an
// `_h` twin taking a `vector<text>` of `"Key: Value"` request headers. For a
// large remote file, `http_size(url)` then `http_get_range(url, offset, len)`
// reads a slice as `206 Partial Content` instead of pulling the whole thing.
//
// ## A handle is not a connection
//
// `ws_handler(url)` answers null for ONE reason: a URL it cannot parse as a
// WebSocket address. A well-formed `ws://` whose server is down still hands back
// a usable handle, because reconnecting — with backoff — is the library's job
// and not the caller's. So a non-null handle means *"this address is usable"*,
// never *"a connection is open"*, and treating null as "the server is down" is
// the mistake this shape invites.

fn a_handle_is_not_a_connection() {
  down = ws_handler("ws://127.0.0.1:18999/ws");     // nothing is listening
  assert(down != null, "a well-formed address is usable even with no server there");

  malformed = ws_handler("not a url");
  assert(malformed == null, "null means the ADDRESS is unusable");

  wrong_scheme = ws_handler("http://127.0.0.1:18999/ws");
  assert(wrong_scheme == null, "…and an http:// URL is not a WebSocket address");

  println("ws     : null means unusable ADDRESS, never a closed connection");
}

// ## Frames that carry a zero byte
//
// loft `text` is a UTF-8 byte buffer and codepoint 0 is the `character` null
// sentinel — so a blob assembled by interpolation, `"{a}{b}{c}"`, SILENTLY drops
// every NUL. A binary header with a small integer field (a type byte, a session
// id, a length) is mostly NULs, so that is not an edge case, it is the common
// one.
//
// The packer is why this library ships one: `pack_reset` empties a per-thread
// buffer, the `pack_u8` / `pack_u16_le` / `pack_u32_le` calls append in wire
// order, and `pack_take` hands the bytes back AND resets. So `take` is a MOVE,
// not a read — a second take answers nothing, and the bytes are only valid until
// the next one, so send or copy before building the next frame.
//
// Measure with `size()` (bytes). `len()` counts characters and will disagree.

fn frames_that_carry_a_zero_byte() {
  pack_reset();
  pack_u8(1);
  pack_u8(0);          // the field interpolation loses
  pack_u8(2);
  blob = pack_take();
  assert(blob.size() == 3, "all three bytes survive the builder: {blob.size()}");

  one = 1 as character;
  zero = 0 as character;
  two = 2 as character;
  interpolated = "{one}{zero}{two}";
  assert(interpolated.size() == 2,
         "the same three by interpolation: {interpolated.size()} bytes, and nothing said so");

  again = pack_take();
  assert(again.size() == 0, "take is a MOVE — a second one answers nothing");

  println("pack   : 3 bytes built, 2 by interpolation — the NUL is the difference");
}

// ## Reading one byte back
//
// `byte_at(idx, buf)` — note the argument order, which is `(index, text)` and
// not the `(text, index)` every other accessor takes. It is fixed by what the
// C-ABI auto-marshal supports, so it will not change to match.
//
// Out of range answers **-1**, and that matters BECAUSE 0 is a value the buffer
// legitimately carries. Using `== 0` as a bounds check reads a real NUL field as
// the end of the frame AND reads an overrun as a NUL field — wrong in both
// directions, in exactly the binary protocols this helper exists for. Test `< 0`.

fn reading_one_byte_back() {
  pack_reset();
  pack_u8(65);
  pack_u8(0);
  buf = pack_take();

  assert(byte_at(0, buf) == 65, "byte 0 is 'A'");
  assert(byte_at(1, buf) == 0, "byte 1 is an honest NUL field, value 0");
  assert(byte_at(2, buf) == -1, "one past the end is -1, not 0");
  assert(byte_at(-1, buf) == -1, "a negative index is -1 too");
  assert(byte_at(2, buf) < 0, "`< 0` separates an overrun from a NUL field");

  println("byte_at: (index, buf), and -1 for out of range — never 0");
}

